  "Question": "If \\(f(x) = ax + b\\) and \\(f^{-1}(x) = bx + a\\) with \\(a\\) and \\(b\\) real, what is the value of \\(a + b?\\)",
  "Question Type": "text",
  "Choices": {
    "A": "\\(-2\\)",
    "B": "\\(-1\\)",
    "C": "\\(0\\)",
    "D": "\\(1\\)",
    "E": "\\(2\\)"
  },
  "Choices Type": "text",
  "Correct Answer": "A",
  "Solution": "An inverse undoes the original function, so composing the two formulas gives an identity that holds for every real \\(x\\). Also, \\(a\\neq0\\), since a constant function cannot have an inverse on the reals. We have\n\\[\nf(f^{-1}(x))=a(bx+a)+b=abx+a^2+b=x.\n\\]\nThe coefficient of \\(x\\) must be \\(1\\), and the constant term must be \\(0\\). Hence\n\\[\nab=1,\\qquad b=-a^2.\n\\]\nSubstituting the second equation into the first gives \\(-a^3=1\\), so the real value of \\(a\\) is \\(-1\\). Then \\(b=-1\\) as well.\n\nThese values give \\(f(x)=-x-1\\), and applying this function twice yields \\(-(-x-1)-1=x\\), verifying the inverse condition. Thus\n\\[\na+b=\\boxed{-2},\n\\]\nwhich is choice \\(\\mathrm{A}\\).",
